// std.data.collections holds the general-purpose data structures.
//
// One file per family, each with a `<name>_test.adm` suite beside it:
//
//     list, linkedlist, deque, queue, stack, ringbuffer   sequences
//     bitarray                                            BitArray, BitSet
//     hashmap, sortedmap, orderedmap, multimap, hashtable  maps (Entry, fnv1a)
//     set                                                 Set, HashSet, SortedSet, OrderedSet, MultiSet, DisjointSet
//     heap                                                Heap, MinHeap, MaxHeap, PriorityQueue
//     tree, bst, avltree, redblacktree, btree, bplustree   trees
//     trie                                                Trie, RadixTree
//     graph                                               Graph, DirectedGraph, WeightedGraph
//     sparse                                              SparseArray, SparseSet, SparseMatrix
//     bloomfilter, lrucache
//
// sort and search are submodules: `sort(list)` with a SortMethod, and
// `search(list, value)` with a SearchMethod.
//
// Keys of the hash-based collections are whatever a `map` takes as a key; keys of the
// ordered collections are `K: Comparable<K>` (std.data.collections.sort), which
// numbers, strings, chars, durations, times and any type declaring
// `compare(other) int` satisfy. Every collection implements `iterate()`, so it
// can drive a `for let v in collection` loop.

partial module std.data.collections {}
